      "id": 1,
      "text": "Port Neches was founded in 1834 by Joseph Grigsby, who named the settlement after himself.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.ci.port-neches.tx.us/visitors/about_port_neches/city_history.php"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1901 the community of Grigsby’s Bluff officially became Port Neches as the petroleum industry began to boom.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.ci.port-neches.tx.us/visitors/about_port_neches/city_history.php"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "During the Civil War, Confederate Fort Grigsby guarded the Neches River for nine months after Sabine Pass defenses were abandoned.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.ci.port-neches.tx.us/visitors/about_port_neches/city_history.php"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Effie & Wilton Hebert Public Library, opened in 1982, traces its roots to a 1930 bookmobile service that served Port Neches and nearby towns.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17,
        19
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Port_Neches%2C_Texas"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Port Neches is the birthplace of country legend Tex Ritter, whose gravesite lies in Oak Bluff Memorial Park.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Port_Neches%2C_Texas"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "In 2013, oral historians Nola Martin and Beverly Evans recorded 42 interviews with 75 residents, preserving 17 hours of Port Neches memories.",
